#{[{:activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186158355} :activity/id #uuid "7117d1d2-5836-549a-9885-24bb3251be4d" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048628 :topic/id #uuid "afd039a1-cbf1-5601-a4b7-0b332dbb15e4" :topic/math-academy-id 2926} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186158355 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186158438 :tutorial/content "In a previous lesson, we learned how to *find the roots* of a function using *Newton’s method*. That is, we solved equations of the form\n\n$$\nf(x) = 0\n$$\n\nby starting with an initial guess and refining it step by step using the update rule\n\n$$\nx_{n+1} = x_n - \\dfrac{f(x_n)}{f'(x_n)}\n$$\n\nNow, we turn to a closely related problem of *finding a local minimum or maximum* of a function $f(x)$. Recall that these may occur at points where the derivative $f'(x)$ is equal to zero. In other words, we are looking for the roots of the function's derivative.\n\nEarlier, we approximated the function by a straight line near our current guess. Here, instead of using a linear approximation, we assume that the function $f(x)$ behaves like a *parabola* near our current guess. That’s a reasonable assumption if $f$ is smooth.\n\nTo make this idea precise, we use a second-order Taylor approximation, $F(x){:}$\n\n$$\nF(x) = f(x_n) + f'(x_n)(x - x_n) + \\dfrac{1}{2} f''(x_n)(x - x_n)^2\n$$\n\nTo find the minimum or maximum, we first need to take the derivative of this approximation, which is\n\n$$\nF'(x) = f'(x_n) + f''(x_n)(x - x_n)\n$$\n\nWe then need to set $F'(x) =0$:\n\n$$\nf'(x_n) + f''(x_n)(x - x_n) = 0\n$$\n\nFinally, we solve for $x$, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nf^{″}(x_{n})(x - x_{n}) &= -f^{′}(x_{n}) \\\\\nx - x_{n} &= - (f^{′}(x_{n}))/(f^{″}(x_{n})) \\\\\nx &= x_{n} - (f^{′}(x_{n}))/(f^{″}(x_{n}))\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, just like in root-finding, we start with an initial guess and improve it step by step. But this time, we apply Newton’s method to the derivative $f'(x)$ instead of the function $f(x)$ itself:\n\n$$\nx_{n+1} = x_n - \\dfrac{f'(x_n)}{f''(x_n)}\n$$\n\n**Note**: The second derivative $f''(x_n)$ must not be zero. If it is zero, or even very close to zero, the update step can become extremely large, leading us far away from the solution.\n\nJust like in root-finding, Newton’s method works best when the function is smooth, and our initial guess is already close to the minimum or maximum." :tutorial/id #uuid "0745939b-5be0-5c57-bc01-4f0f87024707" :tutorial/math-academy-id 10738 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "ba6dbaa2-2df5-5b25-ad1e-3045a5009cb6" :step/math-academy-id 459710 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186158356}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186158356 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186158457 :tutorial/content "Suppose we wish to find a critical point of the function\n\n$$\nf(x)= \\ln(x+1)+\\dfrac{2}{x+1}\n$$\n\nin the interval $[0,2]$. Let's calculate one iteration of the Newton-Raphson process for optimization starting at the point $x_0=0.8$.\n\nThe Newton-Raphson process for optimization is\n\n$$\nx_{n+1} = x_n - \\dfrac{f'(x_n)}{f''(x_n)}\n$$\n\nwhere $f'(x_n)$ and $f''(x_n)$ are the first and second derivatives of $f(x)$ evaluated at $x_n$, respectively.\n\nIn our case, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nf^{′}(x) &= (\\ln (x + 1) + \\frac{2}{x + 1})^{′} \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{x + 1} - (2)/((x + 1)^{2}) \\\\\n&= (x - 1)/((x + 1)^{2}), \\\\\nf^{″}(x) &= (\\frac{1}{x + 1} - (2)/((x + 1)^{2}))^{′} \\\\\n&= - (1)/((x + 1)^{2}) + (4)/((x + 1)^{3}) \\\\\n&= (3 - x)/((x + 1)^{3})\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, our Newton-Raphson formula is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx_{n + 1} &= x_{n} - ((x_{n} - 1)/((x_{n} + 1)^{2}))/((3 - x_{n})/((x_{n} + 1)^{3})) \\\\\n&= x_{n} - \\frac{x_{n}^{2} - 1}{3 - x_{n}}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nStarting with $x_0=0.8$, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx_{1} &= x_{0} - \\frac{x_{0}^{2} - 1}{3 - x_{0}} \\\\\n&= 0.8 - ((0.8)^{2} - 1)/(3 - 0.8) \\\\\n&\\approx 0.964\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to three decimal places." :tutorial/id #uuid "8b8920e9-7fa3-56ff-ab8e-2d0283bc3911" :tutorial/math-academy-id 13141 :tutorial/title "A Worked Example"} :step/id #uuid "b9795715-d19d-577f-99a4-49bef78578e8" :step/math-academy-id 459711 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186158357}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186158357 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186157426 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186157044 :question/id #uuid "35283781-7e25-5144-9bdd-77921cf5ea26" :question/math-academy-id "e-16703"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "da01b8a7-bbe2-57aa-931d-3fc1f54e4b9e" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048191} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050483}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186157715} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186157716}] :knowledge-point/title "Computing One Iteration of the Newton-Raphson Method for Optimization"} :step/id #uuid "2a82e30f-f0dc-525a-9d7f-def00a948b7a" :step/math-academy-id 459712 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186158358}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186158358 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186157427 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186157045 :question/id #uuid "1bf35491-4dda-5bff-9a9e-c3e91f9d5edc" :question/math-academy-id "e-16704"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "636351f5-902f-5d1f-8fd5-b7b47c93ac44" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186157712} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186157713}] :knowledge-point/title "Computing Two Iterations of the Newton-Raphson Method for Optimization"} :step/id #uuid "39b82888-3561-5f52-b687-f896a063cc7a" :step/math-academy-id 459715 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186158359}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186158359 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186157428 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186157046 :question/id #uuid "67b4f920-532d-56dc-8b6b-c76d5115959d" :question/math-academy-id "e-16705"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "de81bf51-3ea0-5c3b-b672-d760060cbe15" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186157714} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186157717}] :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Critical Points of a Function Using the Newton-Raphson Method for Optimization"} :step/id #uuid "c7207f91-013e-5d36-b69d-1544259c0ba7" :step/math-academy-id 459718}] :activity/title "Newton's Method for Optimizing Single-Variable Functions" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186157519}]}
